The directory is empty, so I'll provide the implementation directly. Here's the approach using ASP.NET Core's built-in `IExceptionHandler` (available in .NET 8+):

**1. Create the exception handler:**

```csharp
// ExceptionHandling/GlobalExceptionHandler.cs
using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Diagnostics;
using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc;

namespace YourApp.ExceptionHandling;

public class GlobalExceptionHandler : IExceptionHandler
{
    private readonly ILogger<GlobalExceptionHandler> _logger;

    public GlobalExceptionHandler(ILogger<GlobalExceptionHandler> logger)
    {
        _logger = logger;
    }

    public async ValueTask<bool> TryHandleAsync(
        HttpContext httpContext,
        Exception exception,
        CancellationToken cancellationToken)
    {
        _logger.LogError(exception, "Unhandled exception occurred");

        var (statusCode, title) = exception switch
        {
            KeyNotFoundException => (StatusCodes.Status404NotFound, "Not Found"),
            ArgumentException => (StatusCodes.Status400BadRequest, "Bad Request"),
            InvalidOperationException => (StatusCodes.Status409Conflict, "Conflict"),
            _ => (StatusCodes.Status500InternalServerError, "Internal Server Error")
        };

        var problemDetails = new ProblemDetails
        {
            Status = statusCode,
            Title = title,
            Detail = statusCode == StatusCodes.Status500InternalServerError
                ? "An unexpected error occurred."  // Hide sensitive details
                : exception.Message,
            Instance = httpContext.Request.Path
        };

        httpContext.Response.StatusCode = statusCode;
        await httpContext.Response.WriteAsJsonAsync(problemDetails, cancellationToken);

        return true; // Exception was handled
    }
}
```

**2. Register in `Program.cs`:**

```csharp
var builder = WebApplication.CreateBuilder(args);

// Add Problem Details support
builder.Services.AddProblemDetails();

// Register the global exception handler
builder.Services.AddExceptionHandler<GlobalExceptionHandler>();

var app = builder.Build();

// Must be early in the pipeline
app.UseExceptionHandler();

// ... your endpoints
app.Run();
```

**How it works:**
- `IExceptionHandler` catches all unhandled exceptions globally — no per-endpoint try-catch needed.
- `AddProblemDetails()` configures the RFC 7807 content type (`application/problem+json`).
- The switch expression maps exception types to HTTP status codes.
- For 500s, the actual exception message is hidden from clients.

**Example response:**
```json
{
  "type": "https://tools.ietf.org/html/rfc9110#section-15.5.5",
  "title": "Not Found",
  "status": 404,
  "detail": "Entity with id '42' was not found.",
  "instance": "/api/items/42"
}
```

> **Note:** If you're on .NET 7 or earlier (no `IExceptionHandler`), use custom middleware with `app.Use(async (context, next) => { try { await next(); } catch ... })` instead — same logic, just wrapped manually.
